<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yusuf al-Fihri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 759</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Yusuf ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Fihri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yusuf al-Fihri was governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> from 747 to 756, a member of the Fihrid family descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a>. He ruled largely independently as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> collapsed. In 756 he was defeated by the Umayyad prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a>, who made himself emir at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, and he was killed in 759 after a failed revolt.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to the Chronicle of Moissac, Yusuf was appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a> after the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> in 732 and commanded military operations there. For four years he is said to have raided and pillaged the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, and in 735 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a> was then ruled by governors sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> or named on the advice of the Umayyad governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, to which it belonged. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-revolt">Berber Revolt</a> the Arab factions agreed to take turns in office, but Yusuf, having served his term, refused to step down. Like earlier governors he struggled with quarrels between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>, who were the bulk of his support, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, and with feuds between the Adnani and Qahtani tribes of his army. It has been argued that he ruled as a king, malik, rather than as a governor, wali.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Once in power he ordered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, for which Bishop Hostegesis drew up a list of those who paid taxes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>, and the bishop visited each year to see that the taxes were properly collected. In 755 Yusuf put down a revolt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and sent a force against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, which was destroyed.</p><p>At that moment the Umayyad prince Abd al-Rahman ibn Mu'awiya, who had fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to escape the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, landed on the southern coast and took Málaga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. The Arab troops divided: the Yemeni units of the Syrian junds generally joined the newcomer, while their Mudar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> rivals stayed with Yusuf. Yusuf offered a compromise under which Abd al-Rahman would succeed him, but it failed, and in March 756 he was defeated at the Battle of Musarah just outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. He fled north towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, possibly first trying to retake Seville.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yusuf ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Fihri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 755 Abd al-Rahman landed at Almuñécar on the south coast, and with the support of Umayyad clients, Yemeni Arab factions and part of the Syrian army he defeated the governor <b>Yusuf al-Fihri</b> at the Battle of al-Musara outside Córdoba in May 756.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He fled to North Africa and, after securing support, took control of the Muslim administration of the Iberian Peninsula from its governor, <b>Yusuf ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Fihri</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Died in Toledo, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.68,10.1&amp;year=749" aria-label="Kairouan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4805 875 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yusuf al-Fihri"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5255 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5255 1145h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.68,10.1&amp;year=749">What was here in 749 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
